# =============================================================================
# Auth — REFRESH requests (VS Code REST Client)
# =============================================================================
# How to run:
#   1. Start the backend (http://localhost:4000).
#   2. Run STRICTLY top-to-bottom — requests 3-5 are a rotation/burn sequence
#      where each request depends on the previous response.
#
# Notes:
#   - All data is FAKE. Manual probes only; the automated suite is in
#     src/test/java.
#   - Model: every refresh ROTATES the token (old one dies). Replaying a used
#     token burns the whole family (app.security.refresh.ttl = 7 days).
#   - Expected codes: 200 rotated, 400 invalid payload, 401 bad/revoked token.
# =============================================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:4000/api/v1/auth

### 1 — Setup: register Ada (201 or 409 — both fine)
POST {{baseUrl}}/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Ada Obi",
  "email": "ada.obi@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!",
  "phone": "+2348031234567"
}

### 2 — Setup: login Ada (her tokens are reused below via response variables)
# Expected: 200
# @name rLoginAda
POST {{baseUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "ada.obi@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 3 — Valid refresh with the freshly issued token
# Expected: 200 — NEW accessToken + refreshToken; the one used here is now dead
# @name rFirstRefresh
POST {{baseUrl}}/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{rLoginAda.response.body.refreshToken}}"
}

### 4 — REPLAY: send the SAME (already used) token again
# Expected: 401 — rotation means each token works once; reuse = suspected theft
POST {{baseUrl}}/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{rLoginAda.response.body.refreshToken}}"
}

### 5 — Use the NEWEST token after the replay
# Expected: 401 — the replay burned the whole family, including this fresh one
POST {{baseUrl}}/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{rFirstRefresh.response.body.refreshToken}}"
}

### 6 — Garbage token (never issued)
# Expected: 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "not-a-real-refresh-token"
}

### 7 — Empty JSON body
# Expected: 400 — refreshToken required (Bean Validation)
POST {{baseUrl}}/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### 8 — Setup: register + login Bola (independent-session persona)
# Expected: 201 or 409, then 200
POST {{baseUrl}}/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Bola Ade",
  "email": "bola.ade@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!",
  "phone": "+2348055551234"
}

### 9 — Setup: login Bola
# Expected: 200
# @name rLoginBola
POST {{baseUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "bola.ade@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 10 — Refresh Bola (proves Ada's burned family did not touch Bola's)
# Expected: 200 — families are isolated per session
POST {{baseUrl}}/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{rLoginBola.response.body.refreshToken}}"
}

### 11 — Logout Bola (Bearer access token + her refresh token)
# Expected: 204 — no content; her refresh family is now revoked
POST {{baseUrl}}/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{rLoginBola.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{rLoginBola.response.body.refreshToken}}"
}

### 12 — Refresh with Bola's token AFTER logout
# Expected: 401 — logout burned the family
POST {{baseUrl}}/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{rLoginBola.response.body.refreshToken}}"
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Expiry (no live request): a refresh token older than 7 days
# (app.security.refresh.ttl) returns 401 on use. Cannot be probed quickly —
# covered by the automated suite instead.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
